<Annotate id="annotate_demo" title="Annotate Demo">
  <Markdown>
The history of mathematics is a story of both discovery and invention.
Ancient civilizations developed number systems independently: the Babylonians
used base-60, the Maya used base-20, and the Egyptians used base-10.
Each system reflected the practical needs of its culture.

The concept of zero, often attributed to Indian mathematicians around the
5th century CE, was revolutionary. It transformed mathematics from a tool
for counting and measuring into an abstract discipline capable of describing
the infinite and the infinitesimal.

By the 17th century, Newton and Leibniz independently developed calculus,
providing the mathematical framework for understanding change and motion.
This parallel invention illustrates how mathematical ideas often emerge
when a civilization is ready for them.
  </Markdown>
</Annotate>
